Article 20
(1) Regional Government is obligated to protect the fulfillment of child workers' rights in the informal sector.
(2) Child Workers in the informal sector as referred to in paragraph (1) include:
a. domestic workers;
b. shoe shiners;
c. street vendors;
d. scavengers, street musicians;
e. parking attendants;
f. construction laborers; and
g. other informal sector work that employs children.
(3) Every person who employs children in the formal sector is obligated to observe the requirements:
a. minimum age of 15 (fifteen) years, except for the category of worst forms of child labor minimum 18 (eighteen) years;
b. obtain written consent from the parent/guardian of the child worker;
c. have a written work agreement between the employer and the parent/guardian of the child worker and obtain validation from the authorized agency;
d. not employed at night;
e. working time of maximum 3 (three) hours per day;
f. not employed in a place or environment that can disrupt the child's growth and development process, whether physical, mental, moral and intellectual or the child's health;
g. provide opportunity to obtain education in accordance with their talents and interests;
h. employ for light types of work; and
i. provide one day off per week.

